<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St Magnus Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1137</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlendsson">St Magnus</a> Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, was founded in 1137 by Earl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rognvald-kali-kolsson">Rögnvald Kali Kolsson</a> in honour of his uncle, the martyred Earl Magnus. Built in red and yellow sandstone in the Romanesque style, it is the most northerly cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the finest surviving building of the Norse period in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The relics of both Magnus and Rögnvald were found in its pillars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral.jpg" alt="St Magnus Cathedral" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevekeiretsu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Magnus_Cathedral,_Kirkwall,_viewed_from_the_Bishop%27s_Palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
